  "rows": [
    {
      "id": "place-line",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 46,
      "purpose": "ALREADY CHOSEN, reopenable.  The shape of the one line that says where the data lives:  your own name, a kind, an address.",
      "note": "Your shape:  `<type>`, `at` or `from`, then where.  I'd allow both words, with one meaning.\n\nA thought:  `from` could also say read only, which would read true for a CSV.  I'd keep them the same for now, because a word that quietly changes what a program may do is a surprise.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on real servers, in a browser.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  chosen by you (Q2).  Same shape as `task list is an app`, and a library can add a kind.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  the shortest, but Spell would have to guess the kind, and REST and GraphQL both use https.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a place at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  already compiles in English, with no kind to go on  The same guessing.\n\nWhy:  Decided, and it held up on the real parser.  Not offered:  `source` (Spell UI's sources, source code), `store` (Spell's store classes;  `tienda` is a shop).\n\nRenaming it later:  20 lines in 16 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is <type> (at|from) <location>"
    },
    {
      "id": "web-service",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A server you reach over the web, by its usual addresses (REST, to a programmer).",
      "note": "`web server`, as you typed.  Consistent with `graphql server`.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real REST server.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  what the people who run one call it\n\nB:  `family cookbook is a web server at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  the machine, not what it offers;  also what serves plain pages.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a website at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  lay people know the word, but a website shows pages:  it reads as scraping one.\n\nWhy:  Neither alternative says something a program can ask.\n\nRenaming it later:  47 lines in 25 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a web server at \"https://api.example.com/v1\""
    },
    {
      "id": "graph-service",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A server that answers GraphQL:  the program says exactly which fields it wants.",
      "note": "`graphql server`, as you typed.  Plain, and what its people call it.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real GraphQL server.\n\nA (built):  `family cookbook is a graph service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  --  parallel with `web service`  but nobody calls theirs that\n\nB (recommended):  `family cookbook is a graphql service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  --  the word on the server's own docs, so someone handed an address knows which line to write.  Product names are already fine here (`airtable base`).\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a query service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  --  plain, but says nothing that tells it from a web service.\n\nWhy:  The one place line where the plain word hides what the reader needs to match.  This is a change from what's built.\n\nRenaming it later:  15 lines in 8 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a graph service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a graphql server at \"https://api.example.com/gql\""
    },
    {
      "id": "folder",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A folder of files on a server, one small file per chunk of data, kept in step for you.",
      "note": "You said:  \"if just 'recipes', it would be a space at the (database folder) root of 'my' space\".\n\nSo this isn't a web address but a path into a Spell space:  `<owen>@<space>/path`, or a bare name meaning my space's database folder.  That needs:  a database folder in a space, which doesn't exist yet, and `SpaceStorage` under it, which does.\n\nThis touches the `user@org` review you asked for (Borrowed Ideas And Publishing), so I'd settle it there.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real folder behind the place server.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a folder at \"recipes\"`  --  everyone's word.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is a directory at \"recipes\"`  --  a programmer's word for the same thing.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a shared folder at \"recipes\"`  --  true when others use it, but says more than the kind does, and suggests a Dropbox one.\n\nWhy:  Nothing to gain.  A person's own `file` and `sheet` properties keep their meaning:  the kinds are a word rule, not types every program would lose.\n\nRenaming it later:  2 lines in 2 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a folder at \"recipes\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a data folder at \"<owen>@<space>/path/to/recipes\""
    },
    {
      "id": "file",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "One file holding all of the data.",
      "note": "You said:  \"same as for folder:  path should point into a spell space\".  Same answer as the folder.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Compiles, in both languages;  the engine's file place is tested, but the recipe box isn't run on one.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a file at \"recipes.json5\"`  --  everyone's word.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is a document at \"recipes.json5\"`  --  reads as a Word file or a page.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a json file at \"recipes.json5\"`  --  precise, but the address says that already, and it adds a term.\n\nWhy:  Nothing to gain.\n\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 3 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a file at \"recipes.json5\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a file at \"recipes.json5\""
    },
    {
      "id": "sheet",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.es.ts",
      "line": 45,
      "purpose": "A spreadsheet's rows, read only:  a write won't compile, and says why.",
      "note": "You said:  \"what about google sheet, maybe `is a google sheet at \"https...`\".\n\nYes.  A Google Sheet already has its own code, so it can be its own kind:  `a google sheet at \"https://...\"`.  Then `sheet` and `spreadsheet` both mean a CSV-style file.  Two words for one thing is the one catch.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real CSV.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a sheet at \"recipes.csv\"`  --  short  and `Sheets` is a word people know.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a spreadsheet at \"recipes.csv\"`  --  the everyday word in both;  longer in English, for no extra meaning.\n\nWhy:  Multi-word kinds already read (`sala en vivo`).\n\nRenaming it later:  146 lines in 47 files of rules, tests and docs, 115 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a sheet at \"recipes.csv\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a (sheet|spreadsheet) at \"recipes.csv\""
    },
    {
      "id": "live-room",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A server that people's pages stay connected to, so each sees the others' changes at once.",
      "note": "`shared room`, as you typed.\n\nYou asked what `wss` is:  it's standard, not mine.  It's the address form for a secure websocket, as `https` is for a page.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real room.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a live room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  --  says what it feels like:  everyone in one room, live.  Multiplayer tools say `room` too.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is a shared room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  --  `shared` says less:  a folder is shared too.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is a room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  --  shorter, but a program's own `room` is a likely name.\n\nWhy:  Not offered:  `live space`, since spaces are where Spell keeps projects.\n\nRenaming it later:  21 lines in 13 files of rules, tests and docs, 2 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a live room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a shared room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\""
    },
    {
      "id": "airtable",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/tests/spell/parserTests/wordsPrototype.test.ts",
      "line": 58,
      "purpose": "A base in the Airtable product, behind a small server of ours, since its key can't reach a browser.",
      "note": "`an airtable`, as you typed, with no `base`.  (Your box ended in a stray `----`.)\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Prototype only:  reads on the test parser;  the real parser refuses it until the Airtable phase.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is an airtable base at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  --  Airtable's own word for it.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is an airtable at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  --  how people say it aloud, but no such thing is called that.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is an airtable table at \"tblXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  --  one table:  but a program may save several types in one place.\n\nWhy:  Matches what a person sees in Airtable.\n\nRenaming it later:  0 lines in 0 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is an airtable base at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is an airtable at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\""
    },
    {
      "id": "atproto",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/tests/spell/parserTests/wordsPrototype.test.ts",
      "line": 59,
      "purpose": "One person's account on the network Bluesky runs on, holding their records.",
      "note": "You said:  \"'atmosphere'?  See first paragraph of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AT_Protocol\".\n\nA catch:  AT Protocol's glossary calls the Atmosphere the whole ecosystem around the protocol, while `did:plc:abc123` is one person's account in it.  So `an atmosphere at <one account>` says a bit more than it means.  An option:  `an atmosphere account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Prototype only:  reads on the test parser;  the real parser refuses it until the AT Protocol phase.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is an at protocol account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`  --  exact, works for any host.  `at` twice in a row reads oddly, but it does parse.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is a bluesky account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`  --  the name people know, but untrue of an account on another host.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is an atproto account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`  --  fixes the doubled `at`, with a word only developers use.\n\nWhy:  Not built yet, so the cheapest row on the page to change later.\n\nRenaming it later:  0 lines in 0 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is an at protocol account at \"did:plc:abc123\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is an (atmoshphere|ATmosphere) at \"did:plc:abc123\""
    },
    {
      "id": "atproto-each",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/tests/spell/parserTests/wordsPrototype.test.ts",
      "line": 206,
      "purpose": "No one address:  each person's records live in their own account, and a read puts everyone's together.",
      "note": "`a personal atmosphere`:  reads well.  Where it goes has no address to give, since each person brings their own, so I'd drop the `at \"...\"`.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Prototype only:  the one place line that doesn't fit `is a ... at`, so it has a rule of its own.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is each person's own at protocol account:`  --  says who holds what, with no address to invent.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is an at protocol account for each person:`  --  keeps `is an ...`, which is closer to the other kinds.\n\nC:  `each person keeps a family cookbook in their at protocol account:`  --  the plainest sentence, but it no longer starts with the place's name, which every other line does.\n\nWhy:  Keep until the phase is built, then look at it again in a real program.\n\nRenaming it later:  0 lines in 0 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is each person's own at protocol account:",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a personal atmosphere at \"...\""
    },
    {
      "id": "this-device",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "purpose": "Kept on the machine in front of you, with no server at all.",
      "note": "You said:  \"doesn't say that it's a database\".  `a database on this device`, as you typed.\n\nWith `an in memory database`, `database` becomes a word of its own, so a later `a database at ...` would read naturally.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is this device`  --  what phones and apps say (`save to this device`), and true on any screen.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is this browser`  --  more exact:  another browser on the same machine won't see it, and the example's own comment says browser.  A word for a program that may not stay in one.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is this computer`  --  untrue on a phone\n\nWhy:  Keep, though it's a close call with `this browser`.\n\nRenaming it later:  19 lines in 13 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is this device",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a database on this device"
    },
    {
      "id": "in-memory",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "purpose": "Kept only while the page is open;  gone on reload.  Mostly for tests and trying a program out.",
      "note": "`an in memory database`, as you typed.  Same word as this device.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Runs:  the parser's own tests use it.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is in memory`  --  exact  but it's a programmer's phrase.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is temporary`  --  plain, and says the thing that matters.  But it's a kind of word, not a place, and the survey didn't weigh it.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is nowhere`  --  funny and honest\n\nWhy:  A type with no place is already kept in memory, so few people will ever write this.\n\nRenaming it later:  38 lines in 25 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is in memory",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is an in memory database"
    },
    {
      "id": "cloud",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "purpose": "A place Spell would host for you, so a first program needs no setup.",
      "note": "You said:  \"not sure how this differs from e.g. \", and the sentence stopped there, so tell me the rest.\n\nThe difference as planned:  the cloud is a place Spell hosts, so there's nothing to set up.\n\nA catch:  `is saved in` is what a type says today (`it is saved in the family cookbook`).  Using it for a place too gives one phrase two jobs.  `family cookbook is a database in the cloud` would match the two rows above.\n\nBefore:\n\nToday:  Refused for now:  it reads, and says the cloud isn't here yet;  that phase is after the other back-ends.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is the cloud`  --  your own sentence.  A survey found people's picture of it is fuzzy and they use it anyway, as long as nothing makes them manage it.\n\nB:  `family cookbook is online`  --  plain, but every other place here is online too.\n\nC:  `family cookbook is the spell cloud`  --  says whose it is, and needs a brand decision first.\n\nWhy:  Keep.  Also meant to work with no place line, `- it is saved in the cloud`, which doesn't compile yet.\n\nRenaming it later:  2 lines in 2 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is the cloud",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is saved in the cloud"
    },
    {
      "id": "place-type",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/CreatePlace.ts",
      "line": 29,
      "purpose": "The word for the idea itself, which a person meets when a library adds a kind of place.",
      "note": "Today:  Half built:  English compiles and is tested.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `a supabase project is a place`  --  plain, works for this device and a server alike\n\nB:  `a supabase project is a home`  --  warm, but reads oddly for a server, and every program would lose the name `home`.\n\nC:  `a supabase project is a service`  --  fine for servers, untrue of this device or a file.\n\nWhy:  Not offered:  `source`, `store`, `record`, `sync`, `backend`, all ruled out in the word list.  Spell only used `place` inside code before.\n\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 5 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "a supabase project is a place",
      "recommended": "a supabase project is a place"
    },
    {
      "id": "are-at",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 11,
      "purpose": "Where this kind of thing is on that server, when it isn't where you'd expect.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles;  no test runs it against a server with its own address.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- recipes are at \"/dishes\"`  --  the plural of the place line's own `at`.\n\nB:  `- recipes live at \"/dishes\"`  --  warmer, but `vive en` was why `lives in` lost for the type's own line.\n\nC:  `- its recipes are at \"/dishes\"`  --  says whose, like the place's other details, but can read as a property being declared.\n\nWhy:  Reads as what it means.\n\nRenaming it later:  12 lines in 10 files of rules, tests and docs, 8 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- recipes are at \"/dishes\"",
      "recommended": "- recipes are at \"/dishes\""
    },
    {
      "id": "is-called",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 14,
      "purpose": "The name a field goes by over there, when the server calls it something else.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs:  the recipe box, on real servers.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- a recipe's title is called \"dish_name\"`  --  a whole sentence anybody can read.\n\nB:  `- a recipe's title is \"dish_name\" there`  --  short, but it looks like setting the title to that text.\n\nC:  `- a recipe's title goes by \"dish_name\"`  --  clear, and more of a mouthful in both languages.\n\nWhy:  The only place this sentence shape appears, and it reads first time.\n\nRenaming it later:  39 lines in 31 files of rules, tests and docs, 3 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- a recipe's title is called \"dish_name\"",
      "recommended": "- a recipe's title is called \"dish_name\""
    },
    {
      "id": "versions-in",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 29,
      "purpose": "Which field holds a thing's version number, so Spell can tell when someone changed it first.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real REST server.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- its versions are in \"version\"`  --  short, and parallel with `its ids are in`.  `Its` here is the place's, so a thing's plural reads fine.\n\nB:  `- a recipe's version is in \"version\"`  --  more exact, singular, per type:  longer, and a place usually has one rule for all its types.\n\nC:  `- it counts versions in \"version\"`  --  says what the number does, but it doesn't have to be a count.\n\nWhy:  Few people will write it.\n\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 5 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- its versions are in \"version\"",
      "recommended": "- its versions are in \"version\""
    },
    {
      "id": "ids-in",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 17,
      "purpose": "Which column of a sheet holds each row's id.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real sheet.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- its ids are in \"id\"`  --  short, the same shape as versions.\n\nB:  `- its ids are in the column \"id\"`  --  says column, which is what a person sees, but only sheets have them, and the same shape on a server would be wrong.\n\nC:  `- a recipe's id is in \"id\"`  --  per type:  more words for the same line.\n\nWhy:  `identificadores` is long, and it's the glossary's word.\n\nRenaming it later:  4 lines in 4 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- its ids are in \"id\"",
      "recommended": "- its ids are in \"id\""
    },
    {
      "id": "key-secret",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 18,
      "purpose": "Names a key the server side holds.  A key written into a program won't compile, since anyone can read it.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles;  naming a key is tested, and using it waits for a kind of place that needs one (the Airtable phase).\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- its key is the secret \"COOKBOOK_KEY\"`  --  says it's a key, and that it isn't here.\n\nB:  `- its password is the secret \"COOKBOOK_KEY\"`  --  the everyday word, but a service's key isn't a password.\n\nC:  `- it signs in with the secret \"COOKBOOK_KEY\"`  --  plain, but says something happens that doesn't, for a key sent on each request.\n\nWhy:\n\nRenaming it later:  9 lines in 7 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- its key is the secret \"COOKBOOK_KEY\"",
      "recommended": "- its key is the secret \"COOKBOOK_KEY\""
    },
    {
      "id": "checked-every",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 10,
      "purpose": "How often Spell asks a place what changed, for one that can't say so itself.  Without it, a web service warns.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs:  the recipe box, on real servers.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- it is checked every 30 seconds`  --  honest about what `live` means there, in the one place it needs saying, unlike the tools where `when` secretly means 15 minutes.\n\nB:  `- it is read again every 30 seconds`  --  more exact, and longer.\n\nC:  `- refresh it every 30 seconds`  --  matches the `refresh` statement, but reads as an order, in a list of facts about the place.\n\nWhy:  Reads as a fact about the place, like its other lines.\n\nRenaming it later:  18 lines in 9 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- it is checked every 30 seconds",
      "recommended": "- it is checked every 30 seconds"
    },
    {
