    },
    {
      "id": "total-of",
      "section": "read",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 136,
      "purpose": "A sum.  Spell had no word for it.",
      "note": "Today:  COMPILES TO THE WRONG THING:  in a real program it still reads a property called `total`, which isn't there, with no error.  The new rule loses to the older `the x of y`.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `the total of its prices`  --  a shopping receipt's word, the same in both languages.\n\nB:  `the sum of its prices`  --  Excel's word, and more mathematical.\n\nC:  `its prices added up`  --  the plainest sentence, and it can't take `where` in front.\n\nWhy:  Keep the word, and fix the rule (a bug, not a word choice).  `the sum of its prices` falls into the same trap today, so it should get the same answer, or a hint.\n\nRenaming it later:  14 lines in 9 files of rules, tests and docs, 4 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "the total of its prices",
      "recommended": "the total of its prices"
    },
    {
      "id": "or-values",
      "section": "read",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/expressions/Or.ts",
      "line": 19,
      "purpose": "Either of two values.  It compiled to something always true until this epic fixed it.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs  and tested in English.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"`  --  how anyone says it.\n\nB:  `every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or its cook is \"Bo\"`  --  what programmers learn to write, and worked before.  Also still works.\n\nC:  `every recipe where its cook is one of \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"`  --  unambiguous, and already works, but people wouldn't write it first.\n\nWhy:  Studies of people new to programming find they say `and` for this, so `and` of one thing also warns, offering this form.\n\nRenaming it later:  6 lines in 3 files of rules, tests and docs, 3 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"",
      "recommended": "every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or \"Bo\""
    },
    {
      "id": "either",
      "section": "read",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/expressions/expressions.en.ts",
      "line": 316,
      "purpose": "The same thing, said a little more firmly.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles to the right thing, in both languages.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `every recipe where its cook is either \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"`  --  Spell had `either` for declared values, and now it takes text too.\n\nB:  `every recipe where its cook is in [\"Ana\", \"Bo\"]`  --  already works, and reads like a programmer.\n\nC:  drop it:  only is \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"  --  one way to say one thing, but people will type it anyway, and it would then be an error.\n\nWhy:  Costs nothing\n\nRenaming it later:  30 lines in 16 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "every recipe where its cook is either \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"",
      "recommended": "every recipe where its cook is either \"Ana\" or \"Bo\""
    },
    {
      "id": "helpful",
      "section": "read",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 16,
      "purpose": "Words people bring from other tools.  They aren't Spell's, so a line starting with one says what to write instead.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles to an error with a hint, on purpose.  Also `query`, `join`, `connect to`;  a program's own `to fetch the scores:` still wins.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `fetch the recipes`  --  keeps these words free, and teaches the real ones.\n\nB:  `get the recipes`  --  already works today, with no new rule:  it declares a new `it`, so people from Shortcuts or HyperCard are fine.\n\nC:  fetch the recipes (a working synonym)  --  friendly, but then two ways to read, and no way back.\n\nWhy:  Also proposed, with no hint yet:  `database`, `relation`, `search for`.\n\nRenaming it later:  8 lines in 4 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "fetch the recipes",
      "recommended": "fetch the recipes"
    },
    {
      "id": "while-waiting",
      "section": "show",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 107,
      "purpose": "What to draw until the data arrives.  Write nothing, and it shows `…`.",
      "note": "Today:\n\nA (built, recommended):  `while waiting:`  --  the word Spell already chose for anything that takes a while (`waits`).\n\nB:  `while loading:`  --  what every app and spreadsheet cell says, `Loading…`.  But a program may have its own `to load`.\n\nC:  `until it arrives:`  --  the plainest, and doesn't fit a verb Spell uses anywhere else.\n\nWhy:  One word for one idea, and there's no `while` loop to collide with.\n\nRenaming it later:  11 lines in 9 files of rules, tests and docs, 2 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "while waiting:",
      "recommended": "while waiting:"
    },
    {
