      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 129,
      "purpose": "Read everything again:  what a button under `if that fails:` does.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs:  the recipe box's Try again button.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `try again`  --  the button's own words.\n\nB:  `try again`  --  and more everyday than `reintentar`, which reads as a software label.\n\nC:  `read again`  --  more exact, and doesn't match the button anyone would draw.\n\nWhy:  Keep.\n\nRenaming it later:  12 lines in 10 files of rules, tests and docs, 3 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "try again",
      "recommended": "try again"
    },
    {
      "id": "add-to",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/lists/lists.en.ts",
      "line": 17,
      "purpose": "Adding it to the list is what keeps it.  `a new recipe` alone keeps nothing.  `remove` and `set` work the same.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs:  the recipe box, on every back-end.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `add (a new recipe with title = the draft) to the recipes`  --  exactly what a program in memory writes today, and half-filled forms never reach the place.\n\nB:  `save (a new recipe with title = the draft)`  --  familiar, but then every program needs it, and forgetting it loses data silently.\n\nC:  `a new recipe with title = the draft`  --  kept at once:  one step fewer, and half-filled forms land in the shared box.\n\nWhy:  HyperCard and Scratch's lesson:  no save command.\n\nRenaming it later:  9 lines in 3 files of rules, tests and docs, 6 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "add (a new recipe with title = the draft) to the recipes",
      "recommended": "add (a new recipe with title = the draft) to the recipes"
    },
    {
      "id": "all-or-nothing",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 112,
      "purpose": "These steps go together:  if any fails, every one is put back.  Not every kind of place can truly promise it.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs, on a place in memory.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `all or nothing:`  --\n\nB:  `together:`  --  short, but says nothing about failing, and agrees in gender.\n\nC:  `in one go:`  --  reads well, and it's about speed in everyday speech.\n\nWhy:  Not offered:  `transaction`, a database word.\n\nRenaming it later:  16 lines in 12 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "all or nothing:",
      "recommended": "all or nothing:"
    },
    {
      "id": "draft-of",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 121,
      "purpose": "ALREADY CHOSEN, reopenable.  Forms are live by default, as you chose.  This is the rare exception:  a copy for an Edit screen with a Cancel button.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles in both languages, on a thing a program has;  the engine under it is tested, and no test runs it from a Spell line.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `a draft of the recipe`  --  you chose live-by-default with this word (Q9).  COLLISION:  the Recipe Box has its own `draft`, a text, which is a different thing beside it.\n\nB:  `an edit of the recipe`  --  no collision, and it matches `Edit screen`.  `save the edit` reads fine too.\n\nC:  `a working copy of the recipe`  --  exact, but `a copy of` already means a copy that forgets its original, and this one doesn't.\n\nWhy:  Keep:  `draft` says the right thing, and `save` refuses a plain text, saying why.  But it's the one word here that the flagship example uses for something else.\n\nRenaming it later:  14 lines in 11 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "a draft of the recipe",
      "recommended": "a draft of the recipe"
    },
    {
