      "purpose": "Keep the draft's changes.  The only `save` in the language.  Of anything else, it says there's no save step.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles of a real draft, and refused of anything else, in both languages;  no test saves one from a Spell line.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `save the draft`  --  what the button says.\n\nB:  `keep the draft`  --  avoids the word `save` altogether, and reads as not throwing it away.\n\nC:  `apply the draft`  --  exact, and a settings-dialog word.\n\nWhy:  Goes with the row above, if that one changes.\n\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 5 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "save the draft",
      "recommended": "save the draft"
    },
    {
      "id": "cancel-draft",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 9,
      "purpose": "Throw the draft's changes away.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles of a real draft, in both languages;  no test runs it from a Spell line.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `cancel the draft`  --  what the Cancel button says.  `cancel` now has three uses:  an event, an action, a draft.\n\nB:  `throw away the draft`  --  what the first proposal said, and it's the plainest.\n\nC:  `discard the draft`  --  the same, as an app's dialog says it.\n\nWhy:  Keep:  a Cancel button is what it's for.\n\nRenaming it later:  3 lines in 3 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "cancel the draft",
      "recommended": "cancel the draft"
    },
    {
      "id": "belongs",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/classes/classes.en.ts",
      "line": 529,
      "purpose": "ALREADY CHOSEN, reopenable.  Removing the recipe removes its ingredients too, and undo brings them back.  Things in memory behave as before.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs, on a place in memory, for saved types.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `an ingredient belongs to one recipe`  --  you chose this meaning (Q10).  The words were there, and read as people expect.\n\nB:  `an ingredient is part of one recipe`  --  says the same, with no new meaning for old words, and one more phrase.\n\nC:  `a recipe owns its ingredients`  --  from the other side, and `owns` and `posee` sound legal.\n\nWhy:  Decided.  In a saved owner, it's kept inside that owner's row, so it can't be asked for by itself.\n\nRenaming it later:  112 lines in 50 files of rules, tests and docs, 9 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "an ingredient belongs to one recipe",
      "recommended": "an ingredient belongs to one recipe"
    },
    {
      "id": "recipe-of",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/classes/BelongsToOne.ts",
      "line": 19,
      "purpose": "The other side, free once `belongs to one` is said.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles, in both languages:  an old word, and for a saved type it's whoever holds it, not a stored field.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `the recipe of the ingredient`  --  Spell's ordinary `the x of y`.  Airtable needs a mirrored field type for this.\n\nB:  `the ingredient's recipe`  --  the possessive, which `a recipe's title` uses.\n\nC:  `its recipe`  --  short, for use inside an ingredient.\n\nWhy:  Already Spell's way, and no new word.\n\nRenaming it later:  4 lines in 4 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "the recipe of the ingredient",
      "recommended": "the recipe of the ingredient"
    },
    {
      "id": "refresh",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 24,
      "purpose": "Read them again, for a place that can't say what changed.  A program's own `to refresh the recipes:` wins.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles;  the engine under it is tested, and no test runs it from a Spell line.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `refresh the recipes`  --  every browser's button.\n\nB:  `reload the recipes`  --  the same button  `load` stays free as a word people name their own actions with.\n\nC:  `read the recipes again`  --  exact, and the longest.\n\nWhy:  Keep:  close, and `refresh` is what people call it.\n\nRenaming it later:  7 lines in 6 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "refresh the recipes",
      "recommended": "refresh the recipes"
    },
    {
      "id": "cancel-action",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/CancelAction.ts",
      "line": 8,
      "purpose": "Stop a running action by its name.  What's already been changed still gets kept.",
      "note": "Today:  Compiles;  an action's name now wins over an event of that word, in a handler too.  The engine under it is tested.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `cancel search`  --  the same word as `cancel process x`.\n\nB:  `stop search`  --  plain, and `stop` is already a synonym for `cancel` on processes, so people may try it.  Doesn't parse today.\n\nC:  `cancel the search`  --  better English, and `the search` is the recipe box's own search text:  reads as a different thing.\n\nWhy:  The article stays out on purpose.\n\nRenaming it later:  3 lines in 2 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "cancel search",
      "recommended": "cancel search"
    },
    {
      "id": "undo",
      "section": "change",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/methods/methods.en.ts",
      "line": 1122,
      "purpose": "Not new:  it keeps working.  What's new is that it reaches a place, and leaves alone anything someone else changed since.",
      "note": "Today:  Runs, on a place in memory and on real servers.\n\nA (built, recommended):  `- undone as \"Remove Recipe\"`  --  an old word, and short.\n\nB:  `- it can be undone as \"Remove Recipe\"`  --  a whole sentence, the way the epic's new lines are, and longer.\n\nC:  `- undo says \"Remove Recipe\"`  --  says what the words are for:  the menu's label.  Nothing else in Spell reads like it.\n\nWhy:  Used all through the built-in projects, and not this epic's to change.\n\nRenaming it later:  20 lines in 13 files of rules, tests and docs, 3 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
